                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a,b,c,d,e,f \mid b a f^{-1}=c b f^{-1}= d c f^{-1}=e d f^{-1}=a e f^{-1}=1\rangle.
          %p
            This is ‘the’
            %strong Dehn presentation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(K)
            for this knot
          %p
            We can eliminate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            as it can be expressed in terms of the other generators. This gives us
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a,b,c,d,e\mid b a = c b =d c= e d = a e\rangle.
          %p
            This corresponds to something neat. The ‘geometric’ significance of the faces is they represent a path that starts above the ‘page’ goes down through that face then comes up through the face labelled
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            (as we set that equal to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in the presentation as we will see.
          %p
            This group presentation is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+transformation"} Tietze equivalent
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mn 5
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f,y\mid f^5 = y^2\rangle
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                    %mi d
                    %mi c
                    %mi b
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y=e d c b a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus+knot"} torus knot
            can be seen in this presentation.
          %h2#the_wirtinger_presentation The Wirtinger presentation
          %p
            To get the Wirtinger presentation of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(K)
          %p Suppose the crossing looks like
          %div{:style => "text-align:center"}
            %svg{:height => "214", "se:nonce" => "63639", :width => "214", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com"}
              %g
                %title Layer 1
                %line#svg_75641_1{:fill => "none", "marker-end" => "url(#se_marker_end_svg_75641_1)", :stroke => "#7f0000", "stroke-width" => "4", :x1 => "102", :x2 => "102", :y1 => "212", :y2 => "12"}
                %rect#svg_75641_3{:fill => "#ffffff", :height => "17", :stroke => "#7f0000", "stroke-width" => "0", :width => "43", :x => "81", :y => "104"}
                %line#svg_75641_2{:fill => "none", "marker-end" => "url(#se_marker_end_svg_75641_2)", :stroke => "#7f0000", "stroke-width" => "4", :x1 => "2", :x2 => "202", :y1 => "112", :y2 => "112"}
                %foreignobject#svg_75641_4{"font-size" => "20", :height => "50", :width => "50", :x => "20", :y => "80"}
                  %math{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
                %foreignobject#svg_75641_5{"font-size" => "20", :height => "50", :width => "50", :x => "95", :y => "40"}
                  %math{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_j
                %foreignobject#svg_75641_21{"font-size" => "20", :height => "50", :width => "50", :x => "95", :y => "140"}
                  %math{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_k
              %defs
                %marker#se_marker_end_svg_75641_1{:markerheight => "5", :markerunits => "strokeWidth", :markerwidth => "5", :orient => "auto", :refx => "50", :refy => "50", :viewbox => "0 0 100 100"}
                  %path#svg_63639_1{:d => "m100,50l-100,40l30,-40l-30,-40l100,40z", :fill => "#7f0000", :stroke => "#7f0000", "stroke-width" => "10"}
                %marker#se_marker_end_svg_75641_2{:markerheight => "5", :markerunits => "strokeWidth", :markerwidth => "5", :orient => "auto", :refx => "50", :refy => "50", :viewbox => "0 0 100 100"}
                  %path#svg_63639_2{:d => "m100,50l-100,40l30,-40l-30,-40l100,40z", :fill => "#7f0000", :stroke => "#7f0000", "stroke-width" => "10"}
          %p
            Pick one of the quadrants and think of a rectanglar path going anti-clockwise about the crossing. (We will assume we have started this path in the NW quadrant, to the left of the exit.) Traverse this rectangular path and write down the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext arc label
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{arc label}
            if the arc is entering the crossing and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mtext arc label
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\text{arc label})^{-1}
            if it is leaving it. In the above situation we get
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i  x_k x_i^{-1} x_j^{-1}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            The
            %strong Wirtinger presentation
            is then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mtext arc labels
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mtext crossing relations
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \text{arc labels} \mid \text{crossing relations}\rangle
          %p It is worth noting that any one of the crossing relations is a consequence of the others.
          %p The element in the fundamental group corresponding to a strand is the following. We fix a basepoint above the plane. The path comes down from this basepoint, goes under the strand, and then back up again. The direction in which it goes under the strand is determined by the orientation of the strand: to someone sitting on the strand facing in the direction of the orientation, the path will go down on the left and come up on their right.
          %p
            %strong Hint:
            If doing an example, do not throw away a crossing relation just because it is redundant. It is a good idea to keep it and it will act as a check on the final form of the presentation when it has been ‘processed’ through some Tietze transformations. It is easy, at least to start with, to make a slip in the calculation and often the presentation for nice simple knots can be reduced to a form with two generators and one relation. The above trick keeps in a duplicate relation for comparison.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %p
            The classical treatment of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+polynomial"} Alexander polynomial
            as found, for instance in the book by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crowell"} Crowell
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralph+Fox"} Fox
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fox+derivatives"} Fox derivatives
            to derive an Alexander matrix which is then processed to give the polynomial.
          %p Alexander’s original paper uses a method which is more closely related to the Dehn presentation.
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/knot+theory"} knot theory
